# PandaCSS — Template Literals

Opt-in alternative to object syntax. Lets you write styles as tagged template strings, similar to styled-components / emotion, while keeping Panda's build-time atomic CSS extraction.

> Most projects use the **object** syntax. Only adopt template literals when migrating from a styled-components codebase or when team preference strongly favors string CSS.

## Enabling

```ts
// panda.config.ts
import { defineConfig } from "@pandacss/dev"

export default defineConfig({
  syntax: "template-literal",
})
```

## Usage

```tsx
import { css, styled } from "styled-system/css"

const titleCls = css`
  font-size: var(--font-size-2xl);
  color: token(colors.fg.default);

  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    font-size: var(--font-size-3xl);
  }
`

const Button = styled.button`
  background: token(colors.brand.red);
  color: white;
  &:hover {
    background: token(colors.brand.red.700);
  }
`
```

## Rules

- Pick **one** syntax per project. Mixing object and template-literal modes is unsupported.
- Token references use the `token(path)` function call inside the literal, **not** the dotted string form.
- Nested selectors (`&:hover`) and `@media` are valid inside the template; conditions like `_hover` are **not** — those are object-syntax-only.
- Switching `syntax` requires `panda codegen` and source migration.

